*A Vignette about Chinatown from the Inside*

From the 1850s through the late nineteenth century, growing numbers of Chinese migrants left southern China for the United States. Many came from Guangdong, especially Taishan. Their migration unfolded amid economic hardship and political upheaval shaped by the First Opium War and the Taiping Rebellion. Many arrived with little capital. By the late nineteenth century, Mott and Pell Streets had become a center of Chinese life in New York.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/julia-1.jpg" alt="Map of the Opium War and the route of Chinese migration to the United States in the 1870s, with images of the Gold Mountain and the Central Pacific Railroad" /%}

Family associations, native-place associations, restaurants, laundries, and small businesses clustered around these streets. Laundry work carried many Chinese workers far beyond Chinatown. Some worked and slept in shops scattered across the city, then returned downtown for food, shopping, associations, and company. They worked across New York, and Chinatown was where they came back.

Some stayed long enough to start businesses and buy property. Others entered Chinatown with very different resources. Jonathan Chu, who now sits on the board of the Museum of Chinese in America, comes from one of those families. His grandfather, Joseph Chu, settled in New York in the 1950s with a college education and connections to Hong Kong. While working at the United Nations, he helped friends in Hong Kong invest in New York real estate. By the early 1970s, he was buying property around Chatham Square himself. The family business later continued into a third generation.

{% galeria ancho="columna" %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/julia-2.jpg" alt="Newspaper photograph of Alexander Chu of Eastbank" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/julia-3.jpg" alt="Photograph of Jonathan Chu" /%}
{% /galeria %}

After 1965, another wave of migration arrived in Chinatown. The Immigration and Nationality Act passed that year made it easier for families to enter the United States. Some women found work in garment factories, while others came with technical training, education, business experience, or family resources. By the 1980s, hundreds of garment factories filled Chinatown and its edges, employing thousands of workers, and Chinatown expanded north and west into former parts of Little Italy.

{% galeria ancho="columna" %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/julia-4.jpg" alt="Photograph of a woman ironing in a Chinatown garment factory" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/julia-5.jpg" alt="Photograph of garment workers at a union rally, holding signs that read In Union There Is Strength" /%}
{% /galeria %}

Farther east in Lower Manhattan, another center was growing. From the 1980s into the 1990s, increasing numbers of migrants arrived from Fujian, especially Fuzhou. Many came from rural backgrounds with little capital and, in some cases, heavy migration debt. Along East Broadway, Fujianese restaurants, markets, employment agencies, and immigrant services appeared. Employment agencies sent newcomers to garment shops, construction work, and restaurants well beyond Chinatown. Some workers later became restaurant owners, employers, and small-business operators themselves.

Around the same time, different resources shaped Chinatown through business and community life. For example, Jing Fong opened on Elizabeth Street in 1978 as a Cantonese restaurant. In 1980, after the original owners ran into financial trouble, Shui Ling Lam became the majority shareholder and kept the restaurant running. In 1993, Jing Fong moved into a much larger space at 20 Elizabeth Street. The dining hall was more than a place to eat: it hosted weddings, family associations, banquets, and community events, and workers also organized there. As the garment industry declined, restaurants like Jing Fong became important places of work and gathering. During the pandemic, sales fell sharply, and Jing Fong went a year without paying base rent at 20 Elizabeth Street. The building’s landlords were Alex and Jonathan Chu, Joseph Chu’s son and grandson. Workers and activists blamed the landlords, while Jonathan Chu pointed to a year of unpaid rent. In 2021, the large dining hall closed. Jing Fong later reopened in a much smaller space on Centre Street. The address changed. The scale changed. Jing Fong remained an important part of Chinatown’s social life.

They came at different moments, from different places, and with different resources. Some arrived with little capital. Others came with education, business experience, or connections. Some built businesses and property over time, while others had to begin again after arriving in New York. Their lives gathered around different streets and different centers. One name held them together: Chinatown.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/julia-6.jpg" alt="Map of Lower Manhattan marking Chinatown’s centers: Cantonese from Taishan in the late 1800s, Hong Kong and Cantonese from 1965, the Cantonese expansion of the 1980s, and Fujianese from the 1980s to the 1990s" /%}
